            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Consider a prime $p$ of the form $4 t+3$. Show that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ iff $-a$ has order $(p-1) / 2$.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"number_theory\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Let \\\\(m=(p-1)/2=2t+1\\\\), which is odd.\\n\\nSuppose first that \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\). Then \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1=2m\\\\). Hence \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), while \\\\(a^m\\\\not\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Since \\\\((a^m)^2\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\) and \\\\(p\\\\) is prime, it follows that \\\\(a^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Because \\\\(m\\\\) is odd,\\n\\\\[\\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\\\equiv(-1)(-1)\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nIf \\\\((-a)^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), then \\\\(a^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Thus \\\\(2m\\\\mid2n\\\\), so \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\). Therefore \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2\\\\).\\n\\nConversely, suppose \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m\\\\), and put \\\\(b=-a\\\\). Then \\\\(b^m\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Since \\\\(m\\\\) is odd,\\n\\\\[\\na^m=(-b)^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p,\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Now suppose \\\\(a^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Then \\\\(b^{2n}=a^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), and hence \\\\(m\\\\mid2n\\\\). As \\\\(m\\\\) is odd, \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\); write \\\\(n=qm\\\\). We then have\\n\\\\[\\n1\\\\equiv a^n=(a^m)^q\\\\equiv(-1)^q\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause \\\\(p\\\\) is odd, \\\\(q\\\\) must be even. Consequently \\\\(2m\\\\mid n\\\\). Since \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), this proves \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m=p-1\\\\). Thus \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\).\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "id": "01a094f9-176a-7de0-976c-8a2f85faa0e8",
          "type": "userMessage"
        },
